{"parentUuid":"df1a8807-3aa5-4f28-92c0-f4f5b6d5b085","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdbNY1UtmDLYGBL8mX2GR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Reload the app now (Cmd+R) before touching anything in the Epics pane.** Disk is correct — 1 Epic — but the renderer still holds all 8 in memory, and `persistActiveIndex` (`promptSessions.ts:135`) rewrites `active-index.json` from *in-memory* state on every mutation. One pin, one send, one event append and all 8 deleted Epics get written back over my cleanup. I've snapshotted the good state to the scratchpad as insurance.\n\n## Why you see 8 Open\n\nConfirmed, not a theory:\n\n- `hydrate()` (`promptSessions.ts:282`) only **adds** — `newIds` is filtered to ids the store doesn't already have, and it early-returns when there are none. There is no delete path. An Epic removed from disk lives forever in memory.\n- Nothing watches `active-index.json`. `config.cjs` has chokidar watchers, but Epics don't subscribe.\n- The queue pane defaults to `filter = 'open'` (`EpicQueueControls.tsx:152`) and counts come from that same stale in-memory `sessions` map.\n\nSo \"8 Open\" is the pre-cleanup snapshot from when the app booted at 20:26. A reload fixes the display and is also the only thing that stops the clobber.\n\n## Why Terminal isn't sticky / context is lost\n\nTwo distinct defects, and I want to be precise about which I've confirmed:\n\n**Confirmed — switching Epics kills the interactive session.** `EpicTerminalPane`'s effect cleanup calls `window.api.pty.kill(sessionId)` (`EpicTerminalPane.tsx:123`). Selecting another Epic unmounts the pane, which SIGKILLs the live `claude`. Coming back re-spawns and re-`--resume`s from the transcript, so you get a cold REPL: in-session context (compaction state, anything not yet flushed to the JSONL) is gone, plus a 1.5s command-injection delay. That is exactly the discontinuity you're describing. The comment on line 122 calls the kill an \"idempotent no-op if the process already exited\" — but on an Epic switch the process has *not* exited, so it's a real kill.\n\n**Confirmed — mode never survives a reload.** `useEpicTerminal.modes` (`epicTerminal.ts`) is in-memory only. `epicsPrefs.ts` persists pins/group/sort/compact and deliberately not `modes`. Any renderer reload drops every Epic back to Chat.\n\n**Not confirmed — mode reverting on a plain Epic switch.** Reading the code, `modes` is keyed by `epicId` and the only writers are `setMode(…,'terminal')` in the reconcile effect and `EpicDetail`'s own toggle; nothing writes `'chat'` except your explicit `returnToChat`. So a pure A→B→A switch *should* keep `'terminal'`. If you saw Chat without reloading, the likely trigger is the duplicate-mount problem — `TerminalStage` renders a global `EpicsWorkspace` and `Terminal.tsx:268` renders another per dormant tab, each with its own `selectedId`, all sharing one mode store. I haven't reproduced this one, and I'm not going to launch a second Electron to test it while your instance holds the scheduler lock.\n\n## One more thing that blocks the fixes landing\n\n`scheduler-machine.json` has `firePolicy: \"manual\"`, so the 4 pending PRDs — and anything I queue — will sit there until you fire them. Separately, the billing poll is at 57+ consecutive failures and the live instance hasn't written a heartbeat since it booted 36 minutes ago, so `when-available` wouldn't fire either right now.\n\nWant me to queue these as PRDs via `/develop` (the kill-on-unmount fix, hydrate-with-removal + a watcher on `active-index.json`, mode persistence, and the duplicate-workspace mount), or keep digging on the unreproduced mode-reset first?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2407,"cache_read_input_tokens":145991,"output_tokens":1394,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cach